{"id": "q0409", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 3.01, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1799, "eot_read_ms": 24, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What is the Philly Special and when was it used ?", "transcript": "What is the Philly Special and when was it used?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The \"Philly Special\" was a famous trick play run by the Philadelphia Eagles in Super Bowl LII against the New England Patriots. It was used on February 4, 2018, near the end of the first half, on 4th-and-goal. Eagles quarterback Nick Foles lined up as if he were changing the play, the ball was snapped directly to running back Corey Clement, handed to tight end Trey Burton, and Burton threw a touchdown pass to Foles. The play helped the Eagles win 41–33, giving them their first Super Bowl title.", "expert_answer": "The **Philly Special** was a famous trick play run by the **Philadelphia Eagles** in **Super Bowl LII** against the **New England Patriots**.\n\nIt was used on **February 4, 2018**, near the end of the **first half**, on **4th-and-goal**. Eagles quarterback **Nick Foles** lined up as if he were changing the play, the ball was snapped directly to running back **Corey Clement**, handed to tight end **Trey Burton**, and Burton threw a touchdown pass to Foles.\n\nThe play helped the Eagles win **41–33**, giving them their first Super Bowl title.", "expert_latency_s": 5.34, "stall_ms": 27, "relay_ms": 3368}
{"id": "q0421", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 4.21, "n_chunks": 5, "n_concurrent": 5, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1032, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": null, "query": "Explain the best way to swing a golf iron compared to a golf driver.", "transcript": "Explain the best way to swing a golf iron compared to a golf driver.", "mode": "local", "answer": "A golf iron and a golf driver are both types of clubs used in the game of golf, but they serve different purposes and have distinct designs. Here's a comparison between the two, highlighting their key differences and best uses on the course. 1. Clubhead Shape and Size The primary visual difference is in the clubhead. A driver has a larger, more rounded clubhead designed to cover a bigger sweet spot, which helps generate more distance off the tee. Irons, on the other hand, have smaller, flatter clubheads with grooves on the face to help control spin and accuracy for shots from the fairway or around the green. 2. Loft Angle Loft refers to the angle of the clubface relative to the ground. Drivers typically have very low loft angles, usually between 8 and 12 degrees. This allows the ball to launch high into the air but travel farther because it spends less time in the air before landing. Irons have progressively higher lofts as you move from a pitching wedge down to a pitching wedge, typically ranging from about 28 to 46 degrees. Higher loft means the ball will fly lower and land more quickly after takeoff, which is useful for shorter approach shots. 3. Purpose and Use The driver is specifically designed for hitting the ball off the tee box on long holes. Its main purpose is to achieve maximum distance with each shot. Golfers use irons for most other types of shots during a round, including approaches to the green, shots from rough or uneven lies, and even some short game situations like chipping or pitching. 4. Swing Speed and Technique Because drivers are built for power, golfers often swing faster when using them compared to irons. However, because they have low loft and large clubheads, drivers require precise contact in the center of the clubhead to maximize distance and accuracy. Irons allow for more forgiveness due to their design and grooves, making them easier to hit solid shots consistently over various distances. In summary, while both clubs are essential tools in a golfer's bag, the driver is optimized for long-distance tee shots with high launch angles and minimal spin, whereas irons provide versatility and control for shorter, more precise shots across the course.", "answer_ms": 13208}
{"id": "q0426", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "aggressive", "audio_s": 2.9, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.11, "eot_read_ms": 23, "reference_answer": null, "query": "What is the purpose of a shot clock in basketball?", "transcript": "What is the purpose of a shot clock in basketball?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The purpose of a shot clock in basketball is to ensure that the game moves quickly and keeps the pace exciting. It limits the amount of time a team has to attempt a shot after gaining possession of the ball. This prevents teams from holding onto the ball for too long, which can stall the game and reduce scoring opportunities.", "answer_ms": 1672}
